2. FILES IN THIS DIRECTORY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  README            This file.
  Makefile          Builds the simulator.  `make` produces ./sim_q3.
  .gitignore        Keeps build output and the benchmark traces out of the repo.

  include/
    MemHier.h       Every size, bit width and address-decomposition macro for
                    the whole hierarchy.  Nothing here depends on any module,
                    and every other header includes it.  Change the geometry
                    here and it propagates everywhere.
    TLB.h           32-entry, fully associative, PID-tagged TLB.  Entry layout
                    and the lookup / insert / invalidate interface.
    L1.h            L1 cache: 4 KB, 16 B blocks, 4-way, LRU counter.
    L2.h            L2 cache: 32 KB, 16 B blocks, 8-way, FIFO square matrix.
    WB.h            4-block write buffer, FIFO, with write coalescing.
    cache.h         cache_read / cache_write -- ties L1, L2 and the write
                    buffer together and implements the look-aside search.
    MM.h            Main memory: frame table, page tables, page-fault handling
                    and LFU-with-aging replacement.
    mmu.h           Address translation: va_to_pa() and its result flags.

  src/
    TLB.c           TLB implementation.  LRU maintained by a counter per entry.
    l1.c            L1 implementation.  LRU counter, victim selection, install,
                    evict, frame invalidation.
    l2.c            L2 implementation.  FIFO order kept in an 8x8 bit matrix;
                    promotion into L1 and demotion back out of it.
    WB.c            Write buffer.  A FIFO of block addresses that coalesces
                    repeated stores to the same block into one entry.
    cache.c         The two cache entry points.  L1 and L2 are probed
                    concurrently to model L1's look-aside behaviour.
    MM.c            Main memory.  Free list, frame allocation, the page-fault
                    path, global LFU-with-aging victim selection, and the OS
                    aging tick that samples Accessed bits.
    mmu.c           va_to_pa(): TLB lookup, page-table walk, fault, TLB fill.
    new_main.c      The driver.  Parses arguments, builds the machine, reads
                    the trace, runs the access loop, and prints all statistics.

  traces/           NOT INCLUDED -- see section 3.  The benchmark traces
                    supplied with the assignment are unzipped into here.


3. HOW TO COMPILE AND RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  COMPILE
      make                 produces ./sim_q3
      make clean           removes obj/ and the binary

      Requires gcc and pthreads.  Developed and tested on Linux; it also
      builds on macOS, but see the note in section 6 about sem_init.

  TRACES (required before running)
      The benchmark traces are not part of this submission.  Unzip the file
      supplied with the assignment into a traces/ subdirectory:

          unzip BenchmarkTraces-20260914.zip -d traces/

      Each trace line is one bare 8-hex-digit virtual address, nothing else.

  RUN
      ./sim_q3 <num_processes> <accesses_per_context_switch> <trace file>

      e.g.  ./sim_q3 5 10 traces/2026_27_ISEM_CC1.txt

      num_processes                1..16 address spaces, each with its own
                                   page table
      accesses_per_context_switch  after this many accesses the running PID
                                   advances to the next process
      trace file                   the address stream to run

      The trace is walked ONCE from start to finish.  Every
      <accesses_per_context_switch> accesses the owning process rotates, so
      each process sees a different slice of the same stream through its own
      address space.  It is NOT one trace per process.

  COMPILE-TIME OPTIONS
      Five constants in src/new_main.c can be overridden without editing it:

          make clean
          make CFLAGS="-Wall -Wextra -g -pthread -I./include -DPROC_UPPER_LIMIT=8"

      WRITE_PERCENT      30     share of accesses treated as stores
      WB_DRAIN_INTERVAL  10     accesses between background buffer drains
      AGE_TICK_INTERVAL  1000   accesses between OS aging passes
      PROC_UPPER_LIMIT   0      max frames per process (0 = 256)
      PROC_LOWER_LIMIT   0      min frames per process (0 = 3)

      PROC_UPPER_LIMIT is the important one.  At the default there are far
      more frames (32768) than these traces touch, so NO page is ever evicted
      and the replacement policy never runs.  Setting it to 8 or 16 forces
      real page replacement -- see section 6, item 3.


4. STRUCTURE OF THE PROGRAM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Each module owns exactly one structure and one policy.  No module keeps
  statistics; each reports what happened through its return value, and the
  driver decides what is worth counting.  The DRIVER owns the ordering.

  ONE ACCESS, END TO END

    va_to_pa(tlb, mm, proc, va, acc, wb, &pa, &evicted)
        |
        |  TLB hit?   -> done.  MAIN MEMORY IS NEVER TOUCHED, and the
        |                Accessed bit is NOT re-set.
        |  TLB miss?  -> walk the page table (one main-memory access, since
        |                page tables live in memory and are never cached)
        |                  not present? -> mm_handle_fault()
        |                                    drains the write buffer of any
        |                                    store to the victim frame, then
        |                                    reclaims it and reports it back
        |                set Accessed (and Dirty on a store) in the PTE
        |                insert the translation into the TLB
        v
    driver: if a frame was reclaimed, invalidate it in L1 and L2.
            va_to_pa already cleared the TLB, but L1 and L2 are PHYSICALLY
            tagged and their lines would serve the previous page's data.
        v
    cache_write(...)                     |  cache_read(...)
      L1 hit  -> queue in write buffer   |    L1 hit  -> done
      WB hit  -> queue behind it         |    WB hit  -> forwarded
      else    -> straight to memory and  |    L2 hit  -> promote to L1 and
                 the CPU STALLS, because |               demote L1's victim
                 L2 has no buffer of its |               into L2 (exclusive)
                 own                     |    miss    -> fetch from memory,
                                         |               install in L1, demote
                                         |               L1's victim into L2
        v
    every WB_DRAIN_INTERVAL accesses:  drain one buffer entry to memory
    every AGE_TICK_INTERVAL accesses:  mm_age_tick() -- the OS sampling pass

  THE AGING TICK, AND WHY IT INVALIDATES TLB ENTRIES

    LFU-with-aging needs to know which pages were used.  Hardware provides
    one bit: the PTE's Accessed bit, written by the page-table walker.
    mm_age_tick():

      1. reads each resident page's Accessed bit out of the page table
      2. shifts it into that frame's 8-bit aging register:
             aging = (aging >> 1) | (Accessed ? 0x80 : 0)
      3. clears the Accessed bit
      4. INVALIDATES that TLB entry

    Step 4 is not optional.  The Accessed bit is only ever set during a walk.
    If the translation stays cached in the TLB, every subsequent access is a
    TLB hit, the walker never runs, and the bit can never be set again -- a
    red-hot page would read as stone cold and be evicted.  Dropping the entry
    forces the next access to walk, which sets the bit again.

  KEY DESIGN POINTS

    - L1 and L2 are EXCLUSIVE.  A block lives in one or the other, never
      both.  Every path that moves a block between them also moves the
      displaced block the other way, or it is lost.
    - Page tables occupy real frames, are counted against free memory and
      against each process's frame budget, and are pinned (FRAME_PGTBL) so
      replacement can never choose them.
    - Main memory is metadata only.  Like the caches and the TLB, it tracks
      frame ownership and state, not bytes.  There is no 32 MB array.
    - The TLB is PID-tagged, so a context switch costs nothing: entries from
      several processes coexist and none is flushed.
    - The write buffer COALESCES.  Repeated stores to the same 16 B block
      occupy one entry, which is what makes WB_ENTRIES a count of blocks
      rather than of stores, as Q3 specifies.


5. WHAT IS DONE AND WHAT IS NOT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  COMPLETE AND WORKING

    - TLB: 32 entries, fully associative, PID-tagged, LRU counter.
      Invalidation by entry, by PID (process exit) and by frame.
    - L1: 4 KB / 16 B / 4-way, LRU counter replacement, no-write-allocate,
      write buffer, look-aside.
    - L2: 32 KB / 16 B / 8-way, FIFO via an 8x8 bit matrix, write-through,
      look-through, exclusive with L1.
    - Write buffer: 4 blocks, FIFO, coalescing, stall-and-drain on a full
      buffer, background drain, end-of-run flush.
    - Main memory: 32 MB / 32768 frames, pure paging, global replacement with
      LFU-with-aging, per-process lower and upper frame limits, page tables
      resident in memory and pinned, 2-page pre-paging per process.
    - Address translation with page-fault handling, and correct invalidation
      of the TLB, L1 and L2 when a frame is reclaimed.
    - Multiprogramming: N processes time-slicing one address stream with a
      configurable context-switch quantum.
    - Full statistics for every level.

  NOT DONE

    a) HIERARCHICAL PAGE TABLES.  The virtual address space is 18 bits with
       a single-level 256-entry page table.  The supplied traces carry 32-bit
       addresses, so the driver masks each to its low 18 bits.  See section 6,
       item 1 for the consequence and the fix.

    b) l2_write_through() is declared in include/L2.h but never implemented.
       It is not called: L2's write-through behaviour is handled directly in
       cache_write(), which updates a resident line in place and passes the
       store to memory.  The declaration is left as documentation of where
       that policy lives.  It should either be implemented or removed.

    c) PROTECTION IS NOT ENFORCED.  PTE.prot exists and is set to RWX for
       every page, but nothing checks it.  It cannot be meaningfully checked
       either: the traces carry no read/write/execute information, so the
       access type is synthesised, and enforcing a real permission against a
       fabricated access type would be meaningless.  With more time we would
       add prot bits to the TLB entry and a trace format that carries the
       real access type.

    d) NO COST MODEL.  Nothing has a latency, so the simulator reports hit
       and miss counts but not AMAT.  This also means look-aside versus
       look-through is invisible in the output, since the difference between
       them is purely one of timing.

    e) wb_flush_all(), mm_dump(), wb_dump() and tlb_dump() are implemented but
       unused by the driver.  They were written for debugging and are left in
       for the demonstration.

  GREATEST DIFFICULTIES

    - The traces contain only addresses -- no read/write flag, no PID, no
      access size.  Since Q3's L1 write buffer, L2 write-through and the PTE
      dirty bit all depend on stores existing, we had to synthesise the
      access type.  This is described in section 6, item 2.
    - Getting invalidation ordering right when a frame is reclaimed.  A stale
      TLB entry is far more dangerous than a stale cache line, because a TLB
      hit skips the page table entirely: there is no fault to catch it, and
      the process silently reads another process's memory.  This is why the
      TLB is always cleared first, and why main memory drains the write buffer
      of the victim frame BEFORE deciding whether the page is dirty.
    - The interaction between the write buffer and page replacement.  A queued
      store holds a physical address, so if its frame is reclaimed before the
      store drains, the write lands in whatever page takes that frame.


6. KNOWN BUGS AND LIMITATIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. VIRTUAL ADDRESSES ARE MASKED TO 18 BITS -- pages alias.
     CAUSE: MemHier.h defines an 18-bit virtual address space (VPN 8 +
     offset 10) and a flat 256-entry page table, but the traces carry 32-bit
     addresses.  mmu.c masks each address to its low 18 bits, so two
     addresses differing only above bit 17 map to the same page.  Measured on
     the CC1 trace: 169 distinct 1 KB pages are touched but only 156 survive
     the mask, so 13 pairs are conflated.  This slightly understates page
     faults and slightly overstates locality.
     FIX: a 3-level page table.  A 32-bit address with 1 KB pages gives a
     22-bit VPN, split 6|8|8, each node being 256 entries = 1024 B = exactly
     one frame.  A flat table would need 2^22 entries = 16 MB = 16384 frames
     PER PROCESS, which is structurally impossible; measured on these traces
     a 3-level table would allocate only 5-6 frames per process, because the
     addresses cluster into two regions (heap around 0x1xxxxxxx and stack
     around 0x7fff8xxx).

  2. THE READ/WRITE SPLIT IS SYNTHESISED, NOT REAL.
     CAUSE: the traces are address-only.  The driver marks WRITE_PERCENT
     (30%) of accesses as stores using a seeded linear congruential generator.
     Runs are therefore reproducible, but the workload's true store pattern,
     its store locality and its dirty-page behaviour are not modelled.
     FIX: a trace format carrying the access type, or a documented derivation
     rule agreed with the instructor.

  3. PAGE REPLACEMENT DOES NOT RUN AT THE DEFAULT LIMITS.
     CAUSE: 32768 frames is far larger than these traces need.  Twenty
     processes use about 2720 frames, 8% of memory, so mm_select_victim is
     never called and LFU-with-aging is never exercised.  This is a property
     of the workload, not a defect, but it means a default run reports zero
     evictions and zero write-backs.
     WORKAROUND: -DPROC_UPPER_LIMIT=8 produces about 22000 evictions and
     8900 write-backs on the same trace.  The report prints a note when no
     eviction occurred so this cannot be mistaken for a healthy result.

  4. cache.c USES OS THREADS TO MODEL LOOK-ASIDE.
     CAUSE: L1's look-aside behaviour is modelled by probing L1 and L2 in two
     pthreads and cancelling the L2 thread if L1 answers first.  Look-aside is
     a TIMING property, not a behavioural one: the resulting cache state is
     identical to a sequential probe, so the threads buy nothing a simulator
     can observe, and they cost two thread creations per memory access.
     A second problem: sem_init() for unnamed semaphores is unimplemented on
     macOS (it fails with ENOSYS), so on macOS the start barrier does not
     work.  The code still produces correct results there because the
     semaphore is only an ordering hint, but it is not doing what it claims.
     Linux is unaffected.
     FIX: replace with a sequential probe and express look-aside in the cost
     model as max(t_L1, t_L2) instead of t_L1 + t_L2.  The statistics are
     already deterministic, because no module counts anything inside a
     cancellable thread -- all counting happens in the driver after both
     threads have joined.

  5. WRITE-BUFFER FORWARDING IS BLOCK-GRANULAR.
     CAUSE: a buffer entry records a 16 B block address, not which bytes of
     that block are pending.  A load of any byte in a queued block is reported
     as a buffer hit even when the queued store touched different bytes.  This
     over-reports forwards and under-reports accesses that should have reached
     L2 or memory.
     FIX: store the byte offset (the entry has 10 spare bits) and compare the
     requested byte range against it.

  6. A STORE THAT STRADDLES TWO BLOCKS IS RECORDED IN ONLY ONE.
     CAUSE: STORE_WIDTH is 4 bytes and a block is 16 bytes, so a store at
     block offset 13, 14 or 15 spans into the next block.  The write buffer
     records only the first block.
     FIX: split such a store into two buffer entries.

  7. PTE.prot IS WRITTEN BUT NEVER READ.
     See section 5(c).  It is set to RWX at process creation and no code ever
     checks it, so no protection fault can occur.

  8. STORE ORDERING IS NOT PRESERVED ACROSS THE TWO WRITE PATHS.
     CAUSE: a store that hits L1 is buffered while a store that misses L1 goes
     straight to memory, so the second can reach memory before the first.
     This has no observable effect here -- there is one CPU, no DMA, and the
     hierarchy carries no data -- and a single thread can never observe its
     own stores out of order because loads check the buffer.  It would matter
     on a multiprocessor.
     FIX: route every store through the buffer, which is what real hardware
     does for exactly this reason.

  NO KNOWN CRASHES, MEMORY LEAKS OR RUN-TIME ERRORS.
  The simulator runs to completion on all five supplied traces with 1, 2, 3,
  5, 10 and 20 processes and a range of context-switch quanta.  Checked with
  `leaks` on macOS: 0 leaks, 0 bytes.  The only heap allocation in the whole
  simulator is one 1 KB page table per process, freed by mm_destroy().

  INTERNAL CONSISTENCY CHECKS
  The following identities are verified to hold on every run, and are the
  main evidence that the statistics are correct:

      L1 misses (read + write)          == L2 accesses (hits + misses)
      L1 read misses - L2 promotions    == block fetches from memory
      WB drains + unbuffered stores     == write arrivals at memory
      TLB hits + TLB misses             == total accesses

